In article <1995Jul27.022823.4677@lugb.latrobe.edu.au> cchd@lucifer.latrobe.edu.au (Huw Davies) writes:

Huw> We have been using AdvFS for about 12 months (although there are
Huw> problems with it, I like the fact that I can reboot our news
Huw> server system in less than 5 minutes after a power failure. The
Huw> thought of waiting for fsck to grind through 6Gb of news leaves
Huw> me cold) and once we overcame the problems with insufficient
Huw> metadata (hopefully fixed with the latest version of AdvFS) we've
Huw> been relatively happy.

The fast reboot is why we've moved all our user homes to an Alpha
with Advfs.  Going from 50 minutes fsck's to 5 minute reboots is
something to cherish!  We're running 3.2B on a 2100 right now and
we're pretty happy.

Huw> The problem we are seeing now is long delays, especially when
Huw> posting news items - it can sometimes take more than 20 minutes
Huw> (yes 20 minutes) to post an article. Even my users are starting
Huw> to complain...

Huw> I've looked at the news server system (3000 300LX with 64Mb of
Huw> memory) and it's not a CPU bottleneck (typically 5-10% busy, the
Huw> rest idle).  vmstat indicates that we are averaging about 20 page
Huw> faults/sec. I have ordered an additional 64Mb of memory but I
Huw> don't think that this is the root cause of the problem.

Hmm.. before you start looking for solutions, I think you need to nail
down where the bottleneck is happening.  From the sounds of it, your
users are on remote machines and not actually posting news from this
server.  Is there a network bottleneck out there?  

How long does it take to create a few small files in the new partition
area?  If it takes a long time, then what you might want to use is the
'defragment' command from the Advfs Utilities to see how fragmented
your disks are.  If they are really fragmented (say under 70%), then
you should run defragment on the disk for three or four hours.  I have
a cron job that runs once a week to defragment our homedirectory
domains.  When the system starts to get busy, I have to run it more
often to keep up performance.  

For another tool to examine your system, you should look at 'monitor'
from gatekeeper.dec.com, which is great for getting an overall feel
for what the system is doing.  Then switching to 'top' for a process
look is good too.

Huw> 1) Upgrade to INN (this is the real reason for buying additional
Huw>    memory)

More memory should help no matter what.

Huw> 2) Convert back to UFS (assuming I can be convinced that the
Huw>    performance problem is totally caused by AdvFS and that the
Huw>    other users of our news server - mainly networking/e-mail will
Huw>    be happy to wait 30 odd minutes after a power fail).

Yuck.  I wouldn't make the move myself, it's not worth the pain of
reboots.

Huw> 3) Improve the performance of the file system. One possibility
Huw>    would be to shadow the AdvFS across two disks - has anyone any
Huw>    experience on how to do this (if it's possible) and if so, did
Huw>    things improve?

I don't think that shadowing the disks will help all that much.  What
you can try to do is tune Advfs parameters with the 'sysconfig'
command.  I've set 'AdvfsCacheMaxPercent' to 30 on our server.  Seems
to help.

Hope this helps.

