In article <saunders-2807951514020001@caspar.che.wisc.edu>,
Brian Saunders <saunders@castor.che.wisc.edu> wrote:
>I seem to remember that for some odd reason, it didn't work right if it
>was executed in the startup script.  Some people used the "at" command to
>start it one or two minutes afterwards, and it then worked.

Probably yet another instance of sh semantics...

sh *kills* child processes when it exits! The program can avoid this
by using setsid() or setpgrp() (to create it's own process group), or
you can use nohup to do this for it.

Add nohup and check if it works better.



