>>>>> "Jason" == Jason Yanowitz <yanowitz@cs.umass.edu> writes:
In article <3v3m0k$1nh@kernighan.cs.umass.edu> yanowitz@cs.umass.edu (Jason Yanowitz) writes:


Jason> dual-CPU alpha with 512M of ram and a 128M swapfile.  (I
Jason> would've made the swap partition larger, but I wasn't here at
Jason> the time).

I'll be this is your problem, the small swap space.  Once the system
starts running out of physical memory, you're going to run into swap
problems.  For your system, I'd make you swap size to be 1Gb right off
the bat.  In general, Digital Unix seems to like a swap space 2 to 2.5
times the size of physical RAM.

Jason> I see that 3.0 doesn't support swap files.  is there a way
Jason> around this apart from upgrading (if even that would solve it)?

If you have a free partition on a disk, you can add that to your
current swap setup using the 'swapon /dev/rz#x' where # is the SCSI id
of the drive, and 'x' is the partition letter.  Of course, you should
add this into /etc/fstab so it will still be there after reboots.

It also helps to keep swap spaces on one disk contiguous, to keep down
disk head seeks.  

