From: yanowitz@cs.umass.edu (Jason Yanowitz)
Subject: [SUMMARY] swap space problems solved
Date: 1995/07/27
Message-ID: <3v81nq$330@kernighan.cs.umass.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 107033220
organization: University of Massachusetts, Amherst
reply-to: yanowitz@cs.umass.edu
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

[Thanks to:
Peter R David <david@lama.stanford.edu>
pm@katz.cc.univie.ac.at (Peter Marksteiner)
alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
mrave@brutus.bright.net (Mark Rave)
  all of whome provided help.]


The problem:  
------------

We were running out of swap space when there's plenty of it and RAM
free.  Messages like this in the logs:

July 26 11:42:35 meol vmunix: Unable to obtain requested swap space
July 26 11:42:35 meol vmunix: fork/procdup: task_create failed. Code: 0x6
July 26 11:42:42 meol vmunix: swap space below 10 percent free

This is Not Good.

The solution:
-------------

1) add more swap space
or
2) delete /sbin/swapdefault (or move it to some other name) and
   reboot.

The reason:
-----------

As detailed in the swapon(8) man page, there are two methods of
swapping:

    There are two strategies for swap space allocation: immediate mode
    and deferred or over-commitment mode.  The two strategies differ
    in the point in time at which swap space is allocated.  If
    immediate mode is used, swap space is allocated when modifiable
    virtual address space is created.  If deferred mode is used, swap
    space is not allocated until the system needs to write a modified
    virtual page to swap space.  Immediate mode is the default swap
    space allocation strategy.  [I hope this quote constitutes fair
    use.]

The presence of /sbin/swapdefault determines the behavior (it's a
symlink to the swap partition).  

Alan Rollow points out that eliminating this means you risk a denial
of service attack because someone can use up all the resources on the
system, causing things to get killed, etc.  You need to evaluate how 
important this is to you.

An aside:
--------

I would like to apologize for asking a question that was answered
fairly clearly in the man pages (a man page I had glanced at, no less,
but had skimmed to quickly (I just saw all the "not yet implemented"
options and got discouraged)).  Everyone was extremely polite about
it, but I still feel bad.  So, my apologies to all.  My solace is that
I often answer questions which are FAQ/RTFMs in the same way (give the
answer, point to the answer source in a polite manner) so I guess
"treat others as you wish to be treated" actually paid off.  My father
will be thrilled.


Other stuff solved:
-------------------

In the course of this, I discovered that our kernel was configured
with MAX_USERS set to 32.  Someone at DEC told me I should set it to
at least 1000.  This is not really the "maximum number of users that
can log in" but is some magical number used to calculate various
things like (I believe) how big the process table can get.
Regardless, I upped it per her recommendation (change the value,
rebuild the kernel).

Also, I was alerted to the existence of monitor(1), which is on
gatekeeper.  grab the version with alpha in its title.  It's a tar
file, by the way (which you can quickly discern by looking at it,
despite the flawed filename and the less-than-complete magic file on
my Dec Unix 3.0 system).

Again, my immense thanks to those listed above.

-- Jason

