We are currently using c-news and an AdvFS volume set comprising
two seagate IX (9Gb) disks to store the news database on.

We have been using AdvFS for about 12 months (although there are problems
with it, I like the fact that I can reboot our news server system in less
than 5 minutes after a power failure. The thought of waiting for fsck to
grind through 6Gb of news leaves me cold) and once we overcame the problems
with insufficient metadata (hopefully fixed with the latest version of
AdvFS) we've been relatively happy.

The problem we are seeing now is long delays, especially when posting news
items - it can sometimes take more than 20 minutes (yes 20 minutes) to
post an article. Even my users are starting to complain...

I've looked at the news server system (3000 300LX with 64Mb of memory)
and it's not a CPU bottleneck (typically 5-10% busy, the rest idle).
vmstat indicates that we are averaging about 20 page faults/sec. I have
ordered an additional 64Mb of memory but I don't think that this is the
root cause of the problem.

I am considering several options:

1) Upgrade to INN (this is the real reason for buying additional memory)

2) Convert back to UFS (assuming I can be convinced that the performance
   problem is totally caused by AdvFS and that the other users of our
   news server - mainly networking/e-mail will be happy to wait 30 odd
   minutes after a power fail).

3) Improve the performance of the file system. One possibility would be
   to shadow the AdvFS across two disks - has anyone any experience on
   how to do this (if it's possible) and if so, did things improve?

4) What ever the general consensus of the net is (as long as I can be
   convinced that this will be an improvement).

5) Take up some new occupation (crash test dummy perhaps?)

