In article <3v81ga$o05@c4.hrz.uni-giessen.de>,
Arndt Brenschede <arndt@marvin.physik.uni-giessen.de> wrote:
>When the BIND-server (nameserver) is down (and it is quite often
>especially on weekends, cause its not our responsiblity but that of
>the computer center) we have timeouts of several minutes for actions
>that should not need the nameserver, for example login in from the console.

Some of the code in either xdm or the X server is doing a translation
of a hostname to IP address, and consequently it has to wait for the
DNS timeouts.

>Cause the stability of our system should not depend on a BIND-server
>outside our network, we would like to understand whats going on and find
>a workaraound.

Sounds like your computer center should treat nameserver problems a little
more seriously.  In the mean time you might make sure that the system's
hostname/IP address and the entry for "localhost" is in /etc/hosts,
and /etc/svc.conf is setup with "local,bind" for host lookups.  That
may not help, but it's worth a try.

You could also setup a caching-only nameserver on one of your systems
and point your systems at that instead of the computer center's.  That
will help some, depending on whether the entries in named's cache have
expired.

-- 
