In article <3umh4p$amh@agate.berkeley.edu>,
Andy C. Hsia <hsiacc@prometheus.ME.Berkeley.EDU> wrote:
>In light of the recent incident regarding reading README files first, 
>etc, let me say that I have read all the documentation that came with the 
>code, I've tried many minor modifications to the code, and I'm not an 
>experienced cc user.  If I had to guess, I'd say that simply commenting 
>out the offending lines caused the variables to be defined as the wrong 
>type, therefore doesn't solve my problem.  My question is why doesn't the 
>successfully compiled program run properly and what should I look for in 
>fixing it?  Thanks

There are many things that can cause problems, the two most common
I've seen are assuming that a pointer or a long is the same size as
an int.  Pointers and longs are 64 bits on Alphas, so trying to stuff
either of those into an int is going to cause problems (usually a
segmentation fault).  Using "lint -Q" may help here; see the lint man
page for more details.

You could try running the program under dbx, and after you see it looping,
hit ^C and type "where".  This will show you the calling stack so you
have an idea which routine the program is currently in.

If you can find someone who knows their way around C and dbx that would
help you find the problem.

