organization: Dept. of Atomic Physics, University of Stockholm, Sweden
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <3ukf4i$903@uwm.edu>, T.C. Zhao <zhao@bloch.phys.uwm.edu> wrote:
>Wondering how to make a shared library. [ stuff deleted... ]

The following example shows how to make libXpm.so. Also check the information
given in the file /usr/shlib/so_locations.

ld -check_registry /usr/shlib/so_locations -shared -all \
-o libXpm.so *.o -none -lX11 -lc

ALL objects after -all is put in the .so-file, even if they are not needed.
-none switches of -all and only objects needed to resolve references are
ar added.

-check-registry can be omitted. Instead you can (but don't need to) copy
/usr/shlib/so_locations to ./ (where you have your *.o) and link without
-check_registry. ./so_locations will be checked and updated automatically.
(See the comments in /usr/shlib/so_locations.)

If your library is going to be put somewere else then in the standard
directories, i.e. /usr/shlib, you should add -soname /full/path/to/libname.so
(see loader(5)) in the ld-command above. Otherwise you'll have to link
with -rpath.

Hope this helps!

Regards,
		Jonas

