Subject: LAT group 0 - another v3.2 rant
Date: 1995/07/22
Message-ID: <Pine.SOL.3.91.950722202534.8126A-100000@dracula.carenet.org>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 106708081
organization: STARnet, L.L.C.
content-type: TEXT/PLAIN; charset=US-ASCII
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

I'll skip the gory details of why we were FORCED to upgrade from v2.0 to 
v3.2...
But after the upgrade, printers were dead. O.K, so I finally discovered the
/sbin/init.d/lat file no longer contained my LATCP commands (isn't this what
release notes are for?) and found the proper location.
So my printers were STILL dead (most of them). The nice man at DEC support
asks if we have LAT group zero enabled on our terminal servers. Hell no, 
sez I, why would we want to do that? Um, well, it seems group zero is 
enabled by default on the host, can't be disabled, AND the terminal 
servers must have group zero enabled too, that is if you want to hang any
printers off of them. Doesn't matter if you enable other groups instead, 
and never mind that this worked just fine on v2.0.

So tell me, anybody, just what is the point of having service groups if 
you have to throw open the flood gates by switching on group zero? Kinda 
defeats the purpose, doesn't it?

Our solution seems to be use IP vs. LAT. More work for me, but it will 
keep my network manager happy.

Pardon my attitude here. Just another old VMS manager who woke up one day 
to find a snarling beast called OSF/1 making his life a living hell.

Thanks for your time.



