In article <806153650.6742@sps.siemens.co.uk>,
jim <ralphc@sps.siemens.co.uk> wrote:
>
>Under OSF 3.2 on Alphas you can type `yppasswd user' as root to change
>the password of the user.  But it seems you must know the user's old
>password, you aren't allowed, or given the option or typing in root's
>password as justification for your action.
>
>This seems to make the argument redundant, and leaves no way for root to
>change the password of a user without modifying the password file
>directly.

That's the way the NIS password changing protocol works.  It has to
validate you somehow, and you may not want root on one of the NIS clients
to be able to change passwords for anyone in the NIS domain.

You'd have the same problem on a Sun, SGI, etc.  I wish there was a way
to do it, like allow root on the NIS master to change the password.


