>Under OSF 3.2 on Alphas you can type `yppasswd user' as root to change
>the password of the user.  But it seems you must know the user's old
>password, you aren't allowed, or given the option or typing in root's
>password as justification for your action.

NIS is a distributed system.  Doesn't make sense that Anyone who has
root access to Mary Random Secretary's workstation can change any NIS
users's passwd, does it?  If you are on the server, and use /etc/passwd
as the source for the NIS map, then you can indeed change a user's
password as root.  I recommend passwd(1) for that purpose.


