In article <3ulvh1$oqc@nic.umass.edu>,
Kyle Smith (Brook S) <kysmith@k12.oit.umass.edu> wrote:
>  We have an interesting problem.  Trying to crete more space in /usr I
>tried to move the files from /usr/bin into another directorry.  After
>realizing this was a bad idea (and thus moving them back) I found out that
>su, passwd and all other security related commands (chfn chsh etc) dont
>work.  I assume that somehow some security "feature" is disabling them, but
>I dont know how to get it back so that they do work...Any help would be
>greatly appriciated.

You've probably got the protections/owners of the files wrong.  For the
ones you listed, they should be:

-rws--x--x   3 root     bin        16384 Feb  2 20:03 passwd*
-rws--x--x   1 root     bin        24576 Feb  2 20:05 su*

chsh and chfn should be hard links to passwd.

To figure out what the files should be set to, go into /usr/.smdb. and

  % grep /usr/bin OSF*.inv >/tmp/flist

This will give you a list of files that were put into /usr/bin.  In
each of the records, you'll find the owner in field 4, group in field 5,
and mode in field 6 (counting from 1).  

