In article <DBq0x9.18H@info.physics.utoronto.ca>, 
sysmark@helios.physics.utoronto.ca (Mark Bartelt) writes:
|>The output of the following program has a NULL character in place of one
|>(and only one!) of the ASCII blanks.  Specifically, two blank characters
|>after the 105th numeric value appear as a NULL followed by a blank.
|>
|>        real*8 xx(120)
|>        data xx/120*0.0/
|>        open(30,file='bozo.snarf',status='unknown')
|>        write(30,100) (xx(k), k=1,120)
|>100     format(1p200(1x,1g10.3))
|>        close(30)
|>        end
|>
|>If you do an "od -c bozo.snarf", you see the following at the locaction
|>where the error occurs:
|>
|>[ ... ]
|>0002140    0   0           0   .   0   0   0   E   +   0   0           0
|>0002160    .   0   0   0   E   +   0   0           0   .   0   0   0   E
|>0002200    +   0   0  \0       0   .   0   0   0   E   +   0   0        
|>[ ... ]               ^^
|>            This should be a blank.

This problem is new to us, but it is indeed a bug.  It will happen when the
RTL finds it needs to extend the buffer from the default (established at
OPEN time) but the pre-extended buffer isn't completely full.  Characters
remaining in the pre-extended buffer aren't properly blank filled.

We'll fix this in a future release.  You can work around it by adding
RECL=nnn (where nnn is the largest recordsize you expect to use) in your
OPEN statement - this will pre-allocate that length of buffer and will avoid
the problem.  If you need a product correction sooner, please submit a
problem report through your Digital software support contact.

Thanks for letting us know - though newsgroups are not a reliable method
for reporting bugs.  You may send bug reports to fortran@digital.com if you
want to be sure we'll see them.

BTW - you say you are using Fortran 3.5.  The current version is 3.8 and I
would highly recommend upgrading to that version.
