Rule #1:  Questions should always be phrased as precisely and unambiguously
as possible ...

[ Richard Hatherill Jr. ]

| Does anyone know of a tool that will show me the last accessed date of
| my OSF/1 files.

[ Brian Saunders ]

| ls -u gives you last access time, ls -l last modification time.  Use with
| the t and possibly r flags for sorting.

[ Richard Hatherill Jr. ]

|                                  The find and ls commands do not give
| me what I need.  I need a tool to give me a list of files not accessed
| in the last 90 days.  ls cannot do this and find with the atime option
| does not work.

Note that Brian's response *did* answer the original question, as posted.
The "ls -u" command does show access times, which is what was asked for.

After the followup question clarified what was *really* wanted, one reply
suggested ...

[ Michael C. Grant ]

| Well, this should work OK:
|
| find . \! -atime -90
| 
| You might also want to exclude directories:
| 
| find . \! -type d \! -atime -90

Rule #2:  Examples are usually best understood if presented in simplest
form.  Here, for instance, we could just as easily say

	find . -atime +90
or
	find . -type f -atime +90

Note that if you want to actually see what the access times are, you'd
need to do something like

	find . -atime +90 -exec ls -lud {} \;
or
	find . -type f -atime +90 -exec ls -lu {} \;

