>>>>> "David" == David Hassel <benu@bnlux1.bnl.gov> writes:

    David> 	I just bought a AlphaStation 200 4/166 and I didn't
    David> like the PCish keyboard it came with.  I switched to a
    David> LK411 "DEC" keyboard from another Alpha machine and it
    David> seemed to work OK.  Now that I'm using dxterm, the keys for
    David> the numeric keypad still act in their PCish way.  I've
    David> looked through XKeysymDB and Xdefaults but see no way to
    David> quickly change the mappings.  Has anyone run into this
    David> problem and maybe willing to share their XKeysymDB or some
    David> other work-around?

    David> 					Thanks,

    David> 						Dave Hassel

    David> benu@bnlux1.bnl.gov

Here is an xmodmap file which does the job.  Put "xmodmap 'filename'" into
.X11Startup. It puts Help, Do and F17 in place of these SysReq etc. keys,
switches off NumLock and redefines the block above the cursor keys into the
usual DEC manner. 
With this file you can share one .tpu-gnu-keys with the old and the new
keyboards if you are using emacs 19.x with tpu-edt. 

(Still my question to
Digital - Why  don't you deliver this as standard - it is so cheap for you,
and a lot of VMS accustomed people switching to OSF would really enjoy this...)

Juergen

So, here is the file:
***begin file
! Idea : I.Bremer bremer@wias-berlin.de
! Modifications : J.Fuhrmann fuhrmann@wias-berlin.de
remove mod2 = Scroll_Lock
remove mod4 = Num_Lock
 
keycode 102 = Delete BackSpace
keycode 87 = Help  
keycode 95 = Menu
keycode 98 = F17
keycode 118 = KP_F1 KP_F1 KP_F1 KP_F1
keycode 119 = KP_F2
keycode 126 = KP_F3
keycode 132 =  KP_F4
! One key is missing for KP_Subtract
keycode 124 = KP_Separator
keycode 112 = KP_0
keycode 105 = KP_1
keycode 114 = KP_2
keycode 122 = KP_3
keycode 107 = KP_4
keycode 115 = KP_5
keycode 116 = KP_6
keycode 108 = KP_7
keycode 117 = KP_8
keycode 125 = KP_9
keycode 113 = KP_Decimal
keycode 121 = KP_Enter
keycode 100 = Select
keycode 111 = DRemove
keycode 110 = Insert
keycode 103 = Find
keycode 109 = Next
keycode 101 = Prior
***end file
