Andrew@cucumber.demon.co.uk (Andrew Gabriel) wrote:

".. on some systems the treads support is user level only. Which other 
systems support kernel level verses user level threads?"

AIX 4.1 has kernel level support of threads. I believe (but am not sure) 
that Dec's Alpha series running OSF has kernel level support. In 
addition, Windows NT and OS/2 also have kernel level thread support. 
This is not an exhaustive list. 

"On a system which supports only user level threads, would a blocking 
read in one thread block the whole process? If not, how are other 
threads run (without kernel support)?" 

If indeed you do use the underlying system call - read() in this case -
then yes, the process would block. One approach to handling I/O so that 
other threads run (without kernel support) would be to use the select() 
or poll() system calls to determine when I/O can be done. The library 
can then suspend a thread which requests I/O, do the I/O when the 
select() or poll() call indicates that it is possible, and re- schedule 
the thread afterward. To prevent blocking in certain cases, each opened 
file descriptor can be marked as non- blocking. For the case where a 
non-blocking call returns a partially filled request under conditions in 
which the blocking equivalent would not, the library would need some 
additional logic to preserve the proper semantics. 

