From: wdpgaara@lgdx02.lg.ehu.es (Alberto Garcia Arribas)
Subject: Fortran compiler and math libraries for DEC Alpha 21164 chip
Date: 1995/07/11
Message-ID: <3ttpnu$a2f@lgsx01.lg.ehu.es>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 106000899
organization: Universidad del Pais Vasco/Euskal Herriko Unibertsitatea
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.lang.fortran


We plan to buy a high-end workstation/server to enhance our scientific
computing capabilities, and are conducting benchmarks to evaluate the
performance of two candidates:

* A DEC 2100 5/250, with the new, fifth generation Alpha chip (21164)
running at 250Mhz.

* An IBM RS/6000 with the Power2 chip running at 66Mhz.

The "peak" Mflop rates are about 500 and 280, respectively, but obviously
we want to know how they perform with *our* codes.

It turns out that the Alpha chip outperforms the Power2 one by a
comfortable margin in everything except a linear algebra benchmark
using LAPACK, where the IBM computer leaves the DEC machine well
behind (it is three times faster diagonalizing a large hermitian
matrix, and almost five times faster for a symmetric matrix).  We have
tracked the problem down to the fact that DEC has not optimized the
level 2 BLAS (Basic Linear Algebra Subprograms) routines in its DXML
high-performance math library to the same extent that IBM has for its
ESSL library. In fact, it seems as if DEC has just taken the BLAS2
fortran code from Netlib, compiled it with the default f77 flags, and
inserted the object code in DXML. (I should add that the BLAS3 are
indeed optimized and run very well; the BLAS1 routines do not stand to
gain as much from optimization.) Still, such a performance difference
is really strange.

Moreover, in purely FORTRAN tests, the new chip (the 21164), which is
on average about three times faster than an old-generation 133 Mhz
Alpha 21064 chip, performs only about 50% better in the diagonalization.

Could this be a "version" problem? The local DEC people running our
benchmark claim that they are using the latest versions of everything,
so the question is: How long after the release of a new chip are the
appropriate software tools (compilers, high-performance math
libraries, etc) supposed to take full advantage of the extra features
(capability of executing more instructions simultaneously, etc) that
distinguish the chip from older members of the family?

Has anybody experienced anything like this?


