Cathy,

You're trying to hide something when uuencoding you messages?

Anyway. Try using "swapon -s" to see free space. Check also man swapon(8) as
it contains explanation of eager (immediate) and lazy (deferred) swap 
policies: with lazy swap you might survive even without extending your swap
space. If you need to expand your swap, it is recommended that you distribute
the swap over different physical drives, see system management documentation
on how to add additional swap space. In short:
	
	1)
	- find free partition
	- use swapon to add space

	_or_

	2)
	- mv /sbin/swapdefault /sbin/swapdefault.old
	- reboot

In case 2) it is possible that you still need to grow swap space or you have
other reasons to return to eager swap, but see swapon(8) for details as it
explains difference between these two policies. Don't do 2) before you 
understand what it means to your applications in _your_ system.

