In article <1995Jul12.080410.39434@waikato.ac.nz>, ariadne@waikato.ac.nz writes:

>I wish connect a toshiba scsi cdrom to an alpha 200 4/166 as an external drive.
>What do I need to do configure the system for the cdrom (using OSF/1 v3.2)?

1.  Consult the CD reader documention to see if you can set the sector
    size to 512 bytes.  Left to itself it may be 2048 bytes, which Digital
    won't appreciate.

2.  Select a unique SCSI ID on the bus where to plan to add the CD reader.

3.  Shutdown the system and add the device, paying close attention the
    bus is correctly terminated and that you haven't added excessive
    cable length.

4.  Use the console to make sure the CD is seen.  I think "Show device"
    is the right command.

5.  Boot the system.  Watch for the configuration line for the CD to
    get the device name.

6.  Once booted, login in as root, change directory to /dev and create
    the appropriate special device file.

	# ./MAKEDEV rz#

    Fill in the # with the appropriate number.

7.  To verify that the CD is recognized use the file command:

	# file /dev/rrz#c

If the drive is well behaved and complies to the SCSI-2 spec, that's
all you should have to do.

>Regards
>Bevin Harris
>
