I don't know if this will help, but Digital is currently attempting to install a
system for me with Digital Unix 3.2, ase and advFs.  They originally set it
up to use disk services, after assuring me that both machines in the
cluster could access the database using disk services with the current
version of ase.  That was apparently incorrect, and they have tried 
reconfiguring the shared drives using nfs services.  Performance went
down 'significantly'.  One hard disk performance benchmark went from
243.1 to 1.4 (bigger is faster).  To put that in perspective a 90mhz 
Pentium with a 4gig drive & adaptec 2940 SCSI controller running SCO
Unix had a rating of 89!  The application is unusable at this level of 
performance and Digital is trying now to figure out how to get it to work
before the 60 day evaluation ends.  They are currently blaming the NFS
lock daemon for the bottleneck, and indeed if straight file copies don't
seem to be affected to badly, but random access, locked database access
drop off dramatically.  You might check into that area.
