In article <mitchell.95Jul68923@odo>, mitchell@odo says...
>
>Hi folks,
>  I am porting some code to DEC UNIX.  Some of the programs build and 
run. Others die with a "Segmentation fault"
>error in the C run time library start up code. Has anyone encountered 
this problem?  Any help would be appreciated.
>Thanks - Craig
>
>mitchell@suite.com

-- 

About 3 months ago I had some really strange cores being
created when on a Digital Unix system.  They included:

   cores being generated in runtime start code

   cores being generated in runtime exit code


I did a complete code walk through and basically wasted
10-12 hours looking for the problem.

It turns out that the problem was due to the fact that the
file system that contained my source and binaries was a NFS
mounted and some weird NFS cache bug that apparently exists
on other Unixes too was causing the binaries to be corrupted.

The solution was to delete the executable before doing a make.

Your problem sounds similar, but I don't know if you are working
on a NFS file system.  If you're not then this isn't the problem.

Good luck and hope this helps.
