newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In article <booysems.20.000C35C7@telkom09.telkom.co.za>, booysems@telkom09.telkom.co.za (BlouBul) writes:
|> Someone just told me that you should never use Advfs on a SMP machine, because 
|> it will cause I/O bottlenecks.
|> 
|> Does anyone know if that is the case?
|> 
|> 
|> Thanx.
|> Marius.
|> 

	This is not exactly true! What is true, is that current version of
	AdvFS is funneled, which means that its code paths will be scheduled
	to primary cpu of system. With heavy i/o load it is possible that 
	this cpu saturates and thus limits maximum AdvFS bandwith or 
	throughput. In most cases this will not happen, and as all other
	Kernel and i/o components distribute their load over other cpu's
	you will have typically well balanced load even AdvFS being funneled.

	This will change in future version of Digital UNIX (formerly DEC OSF/1)
	such way that AdvFS funneling will be removed and that extends its
	current support for SMP systems and distributes the load even better.

	Many SMP implementations have some kernel components that are
	funneled or have affinity to some specific cpu, so statement above
	would mean that one couldn't use almost any SMP implementation. In
	fact we've seen with real life loads that Digital UNIX balances 
	tasks (threads) extreamly well over cpu's compared to many "older"
	flavor of SMP implementations.

	regards,

