In article <805320887snz@cucumber.demon.co.uk>, Andrew Gabriel <Andrew@cucumber.demon.co.uk> writes:
|> I am starting to look at unix threads, currently experimenting
|> on Solaris x86 2.4, but eventually I will be looking at other
|> unix systems too.
|> 
|> I would appreciate any comments about what implementations of
|> threads there are around. Besides Solaris, I took a quick look
|> on an ICL DRS 6000 and that appeared to have the same functions
|> as Solaris. Also I am aware of Posix threads, but I know of no
|> implementations.
|> 
|> Solaris threads are supported in the kernel, but I have heard
|> that on some systems the threads support is user level only.
|> Which other systems support kernel level verses user level
|> threads?
|> 
|> On a system which supports only user level threads, would a
|> blocking read in one thread block the whole process? If not,
|> how are other threads run (without kernel support)?
|> 
|> Thanks.
|> -- 
|> Andrew Gabriel                        Andrew@cucumber.demon.co.uk
|> Consultant Software Engineer
|> 

	Digital UNIX implements Kernel threads. Supported interface is POSIX 
	threads but being Mach based system there's (undocumented) Mach 
	threads interface. I'd recommend using POSIX p-threads when ever 
	possible.

	Another more generic source for p-threads is OSF/DCE package, which
	is based on p-threads interface. OSF/DCE (by default) brings you 
	(system independent) user level threads and in some systems (like
	Digital UNIX) support will be Kernel level thread aka. supports 
	directly parallel processing.

	Hope this helps,

		-jari

