IMHO it's time to evaluate what has been developed since then...
I see (t)csh as a dead sideline of shell evolution.

>If you like csh..you'll LOVE tcsh
>If you like ksh..you've never seen the features of tcsh!

Have you ever seen the features of zsh?  Including compatibility to
the Bourne/Korn-Shell refering the programming constructs, zsh offers
really impressive interactive features (nearly completely program-
mable completion, builtin full-screen editor etc.).
(t)csh is good for hammering simple commands to the keyboard sequen-
tially without too much learning/thinking, but one should anticipate
getting more experienced and having to do more complex tasks
(or having to complete current tasks with much higher productivity)
- and the (t)csh model of shells is by far too primitive for such
things.

Check out comp.unix.shells; there are regularly posted documents
describing the weaknesses of (t)csh and the differences between
current shell designs in detail.

Stefan
