Subject: Re: Adding external drives to internal SCSI bus: DEC 3000/600
Date: 1995/07/06
Message-ID: <3tglug$dt@peavax.eng.pko.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 105732213
references: <3tf1gi$15o6@news.doit.wisc.edu>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
newsgroups: vmsnet.alpha,comp.os.vms,comp.unix.osf.osf1


 In article <3tf1gi$15o6@news.doit.wisc.edu>, 
   dbkatz@facstaff.wisc.edu (Don Katz) writes...

 >I'd like to get a cable to run the internal SCSI bus (DEC 
 >3000/600) outside of the system case and attach some additional 
 >drives (to use the other 3 SCSI ID's). Is this supported? 

    No.  Digital has not tested this configuration, which is a requirement
    for our support.  Such a configuration would almost certainly not pass
    FCC testing for radiated emmisions.  

 >Can it be done? 

    Yes.  We've done things like this in our labs, where we could stretch
    out along the length of a test bench.  I wouldn't consider such a
    scheme on a system that I used for real work.

 >and if so, what cable do I need and where can I get one?

    If you care about your data and your time, get a shielded twisted-pair
    SCSI cable with appropriate connectors and replace the existing
    internal cable.  Trying to extend the bus off the old cable will leave
    you with discontinuities in characteristic impedance that can cause
    loss of signal integrity due to reflections on the transmission line. 
    The cable cannot be more than about 2.5 meters (for fast SCSI
    operation).  The connectors must be more than 30 centimeters apart on
    the cable.  Allow an additional connector on the end of the cable for a
    plug-in active terminator.  Think very carefully about where and how
    you're going to get this cable out of the system enclosure, and have a
    good story ready for the FCC agent who parks his van in front of your
    office.

    Have you already filled the external SCSI bus?  Lessee, that's seven
    external devices, plus the four internal, ....

    Good luck,

    -- Kenny

