organization: Canadian Institute for Theoretical Astrophysics
reply-to: mark@cita.toronto.edu
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

To briefly follow up to my own article ...  Before I get swamped with
even *more* messages telling me ``Hey, what you really should be doing
is "kill -HUP inetd_pid"'' (I've gotten a bunch already!), I'd like to
(re)clarify what I said before, namely:

--  I *know* that "kill -HUP inetd_pid" is the generally accepted
    way of doing things.  In fact, I alluded to that myself in the
    original article ...

| And regardless, is there any particular reason that the AXP Firmware
| Update documentation tells us to "kill inetd_proc" and then restart
| it, rather than doing a "kill -HUP inetd_proc", which would have the
| same effect (but presumably without the nasty side-effects)?

... but perhaps the comment got lost in the verbiage.

--  Regardless, it's still the case that:

	--  In V3.0 (and presumably earlier releases), killing inetd
	    did *not* cause its children to die.  (I just confirmed
	    this on one of our systems still running the 3.0 release.)

	--  The new behaviour is either a bug (in which case it should
	    be fixed) or an intentional new feature (in which case it
	    should have been documented in the release notes, and all
	    the documentation which says to kill+restart inetd should
	    have been corrected).

	--  If it was intentional, it's a misfeature (IMHBCO), since
	    I can't think of any rational reason to kill inetd's kids
	    just because it gets nuked itself.  (If anyone can think
	    of a justification, I'd be interested to hear.)

--  And, aside from all the above, what I'd really like to find out is
    whether the new behaviour is inetd-specific, or whether it's just
    a side-effect of an undocumented change in the way signals behave
    in this release.  If the latter case, there may other things which
    act differently now.  (Not to mention the implications for people
    who write code which has to deal sensibly with signal delivery.)

