Murray S. Kucherawy (mskucher@vertex.tor.hookup.net) wrote:
: In article <3t31ma$1ful@ctsc.hkbc.hk>,
: Wong Pui Ming <s11976@net2.hkbu.edu.hk> wrote:
: >The BSD way of doing it is so simple, i.e. put local stuff in file
: >/etc/rc.local so they are invoked at system startup
...
...
: Here's what I do:

: (0) DO NOT touch /etc/inittab.
: (1) Copy one of the scripts in /sbin/init.d to another file in the same
:     directory named after what it is you want to boot (e.g. inn).
: (2) Modify the file you just created to startup/shutdown/restart/whatever
:     this new service, as appropriate.
: (3) Look in /sbin/init.d/rc3.d; there are a ton of scripts there named
:     "Sxxyyy" where "xx" is a number and "yyy" is a service name.  These
:     are actually symbolic links pointing to files in /sbin/init.d.

:     Pick a number that's not in use, and make a symlink to the service
:     script you created in /sbin/init.d.  For example, let's say you want
:     to make INN start up at boot, and you created /sbin/init.d/inn, and
:     the number 41 is available; do:

: 	cd /sbin/rc3.d
: 	ln -s ../init.d/inn S41inn

Just a warning...

The scripts execute in order ; i.e.
for f in /sbin/rc3.d/S*
do
	<script>
done

so your choice of number will need to take into account any dependencies
that your subsystem has on other ones having already started.

- Simon
