newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In <3tb6f2$m4a@news.ox.ac.uk> "Silas K.W. Denyer" <silas.denyer@eng.ox.ac.uk> writes:

>I am trying to compile some multi-platform software, using SDSC's sdscconfig.h,
>which is a machine characteristics file. I believe that the C preprocessor,
>/lib/cpp, will define a unique name for the machine & OS, allowing one to
>#ifdef in special code for it when needed.
>
>I am trying to compile this on a DEC 2100/A500MP Alpha AXP machine, running
>OSF/1, and _really_ need to know either what /lib/cpp wants to call it, or how
>do I find out! It seems that I have to be spot on with this or my code won't
>compile at all! Can anyone help....?!

The trick is to invoke cc with the -v option.  It will display the cpp
command and you'll be able to get the information you need.

    ues5:~/tmp 12> cc -v -E test.c >/dev/null
    /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/cfe -E -Xv test.c -DLANGUAGE_C -D__LANGUAGE_C__ -D_CFE -Dunix -D__unix__ -D__osf__ -D_LONGLONG -D__alpha -D__DSO__ -DSYSTYPE_BSD -D_SYSTYPE_BSD -I/usr/include3.11 

It seems that __alpha is what you need, or __alpha and __osf__

You can use this trick on any Unix system to find out which system
specific macros are defined when the preprocessor is invoked.

Dan
