In Article <mackey-0507950834440001@starr.radonc.musc.edu>
mackey@radonc.musc.edu (Christopher J. Mackey) writes:
>I want to be able to do transfers of binary files from a Sun/SunOS machine
>to a DEC Alpha using the zmodem protocol.  I have used kermit successfully
>but it is *very* slow.  I will be moving approximately 60-70 256k images
>from the Sun to the Alpha.  I have been told that zmodem is one of the
>fastest protocols out there.  As of this writing I have not been able to
>find zmodem software for UNIX to UNIX transfer; only software to transfer
>from UNIX to PC and PC to UNIX.  BTW, I would prefer a shareware version. 
>Thanks in advance!

There are only 2 versions of zmodem: old public domain and new, proprietary.
The new versions are freely available, but only work with licensed versions on
PCs.

Current versions of kermit (C-Kermit) are very close to zmodem in performance.
Older versions (Kermit32) are VERY slow. You might want to simply upgrade to
current Kermit software (ftp://kermit.columbia.edu/kermit/bin/). Get
ckuker.VERSION, where VERSION is the executable for the sunos or dec-osf
software version you are running. It can still interoperate with Kermit32 until
you can get the new software implemented everywhere. As long as either end of
the link is Kermit32, it will be slow.

