Hi,

Recently there was a thread on a bug of OSF's cc compiler, regarding the
-O3 optimization option. I have a problem with the *Fortran* compiler that
I suspect can be traced back to cc's bug. Here is my problem:

----------------
% f77 -v nbmod.f
/usr/lib/cmplrs/fort/decfort -o /tmp/forAAAaanbea.o nbmod.f 
/usr/bin/cc -v /usr/lib/cmplrs/fort/for_main.o /tmp/forAAAaanbea.o -O4 -lUfor
-lfor -lFutil -lm -lots 
/usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ld -g0 -O4 -call_shared /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/crt0.o
/usr/lib/cmplrs/fort/for_main.o /tmp/forAAAaanbea.o -lUfor -lfor -lFutil
-lm -lots -lc 
Assertion failed: 0, file ../../../../../../src/usr/ccs/lib/libmld/
cmrlc_produce.c, line 864
0.2u 0.0s 0:00 30% 0+7k 18+8io 1pf+0w
Fatal error in: /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ld IOT/Abort trap 
fort: Severe: Failed while trying to link.
----------------

I have no idea what the assertion failure means and I could not locate
any ../../../blabla/cmrlc_produce.c file (could it probably be missing?)
What does cmrlc_produce.c do?

The default optimization parameter for DEC OSF f77 is -O4, which is actually
passed down to the cc compiler. When I compiled the program with -O0 and -O1
there was no problem. When I compiled with -O2 though, it would *not*
work. So it seems my problem starts with -O2, not -O3.

One solution suggested was to use the -migrate option,
but that did not work either:

----------------
% f77 -migrate -v nbmod.f
/usr/lib/cmplrs/fort/decfort -o /tmp/forAAAaalzra.o nbmod.f 
/usr/bin/cc -migrate -v /usr/lib/cmplrs/fort/for_main.o /tmp/forAAAaalzra.o -O4
-lUfor -lfor -lFutil -lm -lots 
/usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ld -g0 -O4 -std0 -call_shared /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/crt0.o
/usr/lib/cmplrs/fort/for_main.o /tmp/forAAAaalzra.o -lUfor -lfor -lFutil -lm
-lots -lots -lc 
Assertion failed: 0, file ../../../../../../src/usr/ccs/lib/libmld/
cmrlc_produce.c, line 864
0.2u 0.0s 0:00 42% 0+7k 5+9io 0pf+0w
Fatal error in: /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ld IOT/Abort trap 
fort: Severe: Failed while trying to link.
----------------

This is interesting, because according to the documentation, -migrate
forces the compiler to use the DEC C (instead of the DEC OSF/1 C)
compilation environment, which uses "other" optimizations.

One difference between my case and the cc bug reported by Bill Wang is that
I cannot even link, whereas he *could* link, but he would get different
results from the optimized and the non-optimized executables.

I have compiled the source code successfully on Ultrix, AIX, VMS and SunOS.

I will have to use my Alpha for runs that could take several weeks at
a time, and optimization could be crucial (I have had execution time
decreased by more than a factor of 2 between -O1 and -O4 on one occasion).
So using -O1 would not really be a satisfactory solution (unless there
was a way to know that -O4 would not significantly improve performance).

Any help would be appreciated. Please cc: your post to me personally.

